{"id":"dualsense-hidonly","name":"DualSense (PS5) HID-only","vendor":"Sony","vid":"0x054C","pid":"0x0CE6","productString":"DualSense Wireless Controller","manufacturerString":"Sony Interactive Entertainment","type":"gamepad","connection":"usb","descriptor":"","inputReportSize":64,"notes":"273-byte DualSense descriptor with the top-level collection usage changed from Game Pad (0x05) to Joystick (0x04). Serves sidecar profile mode 0 (HID-only fallback): the device is root-enumerated, not on the USB bus, so GameInput/WGI does not apply the native DualSense decoder and instead reads it with the generic gamepad template. That template remaps the declared usage slots (RT from Rz inverted, RSY from Rx inverted, LT from Ry), so the Sony byte layout reads RightTrigger~0.5 and RightThumbstickY=1.0 at rest and win32k keeps injecting gamepad navigation keys (issue #1056). The generic template cannot be satisfied by any Sony-ordered byte packing, and the pinned HIDMaestro 1.7.3 codec has no invert option, so the only correct escape is to stop being a gamepad: with the Joystick usage the device is exposed via RawGameController/generic HID with correct per-usage values (X/Y/Z/Rz/Rx/Ry = LX/LY/RX/RY/L2/R2), the gamepad template disengages, and the desktop ghost navigation disappears. The composite (usbip) profile keeps the native Game Pad usage because a real USB enumeration gets the native DualSense decoder and reads it correctly. Same bytes otherwise; source: DJm00n/ControllersInfo, confirmed by nondebug/dualsense.","buttonMap":[1,2,0,3,4,5,8,9,10,11,12,13,-1,-1,-1,14],"triggerButtons":[6,7],"axisMap":{"0x32":"rightStickX","0x35":"rightStickY","0x33":"leftTrigger","0x34":"rightTrigger"},"extendedReport":{"alwaysArmed":true,"reportId":"0x01","size":64,"fields":[{"byte":1,"type":"uint8-axis","semantic":"leftStickX","center":128},{"byte":2,"type":"uint8-axis","semantic":"leftStickY","center":128},{"byte":3,"type":"uint8-axis","semantic":"rightStickX","center":128},{"byte":4,"type":"